The schema.org JSON-LD context currently defines merchantReturnDays as:

{
  "@id": "schema:merchantReturnDays",
  "@type": "Date"
}

However, the merchantReturnDays property expects Date, DateTime, or Integer, and its description says it can specify either a fixed return date or the number of days from delivery.

When processing JSON-LD such as:

{
  "@context": "https://schema.org/",
  "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
  "merchantReturnDays": 60
}

a JSON-LD processor such as PyLD expands the value as:

{
  "@type": "http://schema.org/Date",
  "@value": 60
}

This appears semantically incorrect for the integer case. The value 60 represents a number of days, not a Date.

Expected behavior would be either no fixed @type coercion for this property, or a representation that allows numeric values to remain integers, e.g. xsd:integer/RDF integer literals.

**Motivation**:

Product variants are important in eCommerce, and vocabulary to support variant understanding was discussed extensively in  [#1797](https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/1797) and [#2587](https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/issues/2587). As a result, class [ProductGroup](https://schema.org/ProductGroup) was added to model parent products, and property [variesBy](https://schema.org/variesBy) was added for use on [ProductGroup](https://schema.org/ProductGroup) to identify variant identifying dimensions. Examples of variant dimensions are size and color of a shirt, or memory size and cpu for a laptop.

A limitation of the current vocabulary is that it does not support specifying the *available values* for each variant-identifying dimension, for example colors: red, blue, green, white; or memory: 16GB, 32GB, 64GB. Related, the vocabulary currently also does not generally support specifying the specific dimension values for each variant (except for a few first-class properties such as [size](https://schema.org/size) and [color](https://schema.org/color)), e.g, memory_size=32GB, cpu=high_16core. Other eCommerce vocabularies explicitly model variant dimensions and their available values, for example the [Universal Commerce Protocol](https://github.com/Universal-Commerce-Protocol/ucp) defines shapes [product_option](https://github.com/Universal-Commerce-Protocol/ucp/blob/main/source/schemas/shopping/types/product_option.json), [option_value](https://github.com/Universal-Commerce-Protocol/ucp/blob/main/source/schemas/shopping/types/option_value.json), and [selected_option](https://github.com/Universal-Commerce-Protocol/ucp/blob/main/source/schemas/shopping/types/selected_option.json).

**Proposal:**

We propose to address this limitation by adding a new **VariantOption** class to allow the specification of one or more variant dimension values. **VariantOption** will be a subclass of [StructuredValue](https://schema.org/StructuredValue) with new properties **optionDimension** and **optionValue**, both with range [Text](https://schema.org/Text) and [TextObject](https://schema.org/TextObject). 

**VariantOption** will be added to the range of [variesBy](https://schema.org/variesBy) (to define the available variant dimensions and values for [ProductGroup](https://schema.org/ProductGroup)) and will also be added to the range of a new property **selectedOption** for use on [Product](https://schema.org/Product) to define the specific variant options selected for an individual variant Product.

**Example:**
```
{
 "@context": "https://schema.org/",
 "@type": "ProductGroup",
 "productGroupID": "L16272",
 "name": "Super laptop, in many variants",
 "url": "https://www.example.com/laptop",
 "variesBy": [
   {
     "@type": "VariantOption",
     "optionDimension": "Memory size",
     "optionValue": ["16GB", "32GB", "64GB"]
   },
   {
     "@type": "VariantOption",
     "optionDimension": "CPU",
     "optionValue": ["low_8core", "high_16core"]
   }
 ],
 "hasVariant": [
   {
     "@type": "Product",
     "productID": "L16272-M0001",
     "name": "Super laptop with 16GB memory and low_8core CPU",
     "url": "https://www.example.com/laptop?memory=16GB&cpu=low_8core",
     "selectedOption": [
       {
         "@type": "VariantOption",
         "optionDimension": "Memory size",
         "optionValue": "16GB"
       },
       {
         "@type": "VariantOption",
         "optionDimension": "CPU",
         "optionValue": "low_8core"
       }
     ]
   },
   {
     "@type": "Product",
     "productID": "L16272-M0002",
     "name": "Super laptop with 32GB memory and high_16core CPU",
     "url": "https://www.example.com/laptop?memory=32GB&cpu=high_16core",
     "selectedOption": [
       {
         "@type": "VariantOption",
         "optionDimension": "Memory size",
         "optionValue": "32GB"
       },
       {
         "@type": "VariantOption",
         "optionDimension": "CPU",
         "optionValue": "high_16core"
       }
     ]
   }
 ]
}
```

Currently from what I can tell `schema.org` doesn't label its type as `owl:Ontology`. As such, operations upon an `owl:Ontology` like `owl:imports` I don't believe are valid when applied to the`schema.org` vocabulary as a whole. Please correct me if I am misunderstanding though. 

Note the `owl:imports` domain/range here. 
```ttl
owl:imports a owl:OntologyProperty ;
     rdfs:label "imports" ;
     rdfs:comment "The property that is used for importing other ontologies into a given ontology." ;
     rdfs:domain owl:Ontology ;
     rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> ;
     rdfs:range owl:Ontology . 
```

My understanding is that most other standard vocabularies label themselves as `owl:Ontology` for instance [shacl](https://www.w3.org/ns/shacl/) and [rdf-schema](https://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#) are both defined as `owl:Ontology`.  

